    "lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For an integer $d\\ge1$, let $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$ be the complex Schwartz space with seminorms\n$p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^d}|x^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta f(x)|$\nfor all multi-indices $\\alpha,\\beta$; this is exactly\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]]. Let\n$\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^d)=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ with the support convention of\n[[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]] and the locally convex LF topology of\n[[def-test-function-topology]]. The tempered-distribution space $\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^d)$\nis the space of continuous complex-linear functionals on this topological\n$\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$, paired bilinearly with test functions, as in\n[[def-tempered-distribution]].\n\nFor every complex Hilbert space $H$, use the second-variable-linear pairing\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_2:=(v,u)_1$, where $(\\cdot,\\cdot)_1$ is the first-variable-linear\nHilbert pairing of [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]. Thus\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_2$ is linear in $v$ and conjugate-linear in $u$.\nFor each tensor product, use the same norm completion as\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]], but equip it with the\nsecond-variable-linear pairing obtained by conjugating that supplier's\nfirst-variable-linear pairing. This leaves the norm and completion unchanged;\non elementary tensors it gives the product of the second-variable pairings.\nLet $H^{\\widehat\\otimes n}$ be the iterated Hilbert tensor completion, with\n$H^{\\widehat\\otimes0}:=\\mathbb C$; the tensor inner product is the product of\nthe factor pairings. Identify parenthesizations by the isometry fixing\nelementary tensors: the product pairing is the same on their dense span,\nso the map and its inverse extend to the completions. For $n\\ge1$, let\n$P_{s,n}=n!^{-1}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}U_\\pi$ and\n$P_{a,n}=n!^{-1}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\pi)U_\\pi$, using the\npermutation action and the orthogonal projections of\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections]]. Define\n$H_s^{(n)}=\\operatorname{ran}P_{s,n}$ and\n$H_a^{(n)}=\\operatorname{ran}P_{a,n}$, with\n$H_s^{(0)}=H_a^{(0)}=\\mathbb C$. These are the symmetric and antisymmetric\n$n$-particle sectors.\n\nThe bosonic and fermionic Fock Hilbert spaces are the square-summable direct\nsums\n$\\mathcal F_s(H)=\\{(\\Psi_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\Psi_n\\in H_s^{(n)},\\\n\\sum_n\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$ and\n$\\mathcal F_a(H)=\\{(\\Psi_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\Psi_n\\in H_a^{(n)},\\\n\\sum_n\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$, with the componentwise inner product.\nTheir vacuum is $\\Omega=(1,0,0,\\ldots)$. The finite-particle subspace\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ consists of sequences with only finitely many nonzero\ncomponents.\n\nFor $h\\in H$, the map $C_h:H\\to\\mathbb C$, $C_h(v)=\\langle h,v\\rangle_2$, is linear and bounded:\n$|C_h(v)|=|(v,h)_1|\\le\\|h\\|\\,\\|v\\|$ by\n[[thm-cauchy-schwarz-for-real-and-complex-inner-product-spaces]].\nFor any Hilbert space $K$, identify $\\mathbb C\\widehat\\otimes K$ with $K$\nby $J_K(z\\otimes v)=zv$. Every algebraic tensor is $1\\otimes w$;\nthe tensor norm is then $\\|w\\|$, so $J_K$ extends to a surjective isometry\non the completion (with inverse $w\\mapsto1\\otimes w$).\nThroughout the following formulas, $C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)}$ means\n$J_{H^{\\widehat\\otimes(n-1)}}\\circ(C_h\\widehat\\otimes I)$, with\n$I$ the identity on $H^{\\widehat\\otimes(n-1)}$.\nThe bounded tensor-map supplier therefore applies and gives norm at most\n$\\|h\\|$. This includes $n=1$, where $H^{\\widehat\\otimes0}=\\mathbb C$.\nContraction commutes with permutations of the remaining $n-1$ factors,\nso it takes symmetric or antisymmetric tensors to the corresponding\n$(n-1)$-particle sector (and to $\\mathbb C$ for $n=1$).\n\nFor $n\\ge0$, bosonic creation on sector $n$ is\n$a_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{s,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)$; for $n\\ge1$,\nbosonic annihilation is\n$a_n(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt n(C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)})\\Psi_n$, where\n$C_h$ is the bounded functional above, its tensor extension is supplied by\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-bounded-tensor-maps]], and $a(h)\\Omega=0$. Define fermionic creation\nby $c_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{a,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)$; for $n\\ge1$,\nfermionic annihilation is the corresponding first-factor contraction\n$c_n(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt n(C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)})\\Psi_n$, and\n$c(h)\\Omega=0$. The annihilation maps are conjugate-linear in $h$; the\ncreation maps are linear in $h$. These formulas define sector maps on the\nfinite-particle subspace; for separable $H$ (that is, $H$ has an at most countable\nnorm-dense subset), their maximal closed domains and operator relations are proved in\n[[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. No physical statistics assignment or spin–statistics\ntheorem is part of these mathematical definitions.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qft-dirac-projections",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], the mathematical scalar shell conventions of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and the second-variable-linear Hilbert pairing of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. In the fixed inertial coordinates $x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$ of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] on the affine Minkowski model [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]], $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$; these coordinates have length units under [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. The parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ and momentum coordinate $\\mathbf k$ have inverse-length units, and $\\omega(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}$ has the same units. The parameter is not identified here with a physical rest mass. Matrix $h_D$ has inverse-length units; the dimensionless spin matrices have no physical unit assignment in this mathematical item.\n\nUse the Pauli matrices $\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}$, and $\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$. On $\\mathbb C^4=\\mathbb C^2\\oplus\\mathbb C^2$, define\n$$\\beta=\\begin{pmatrix}I_2&0\\\\0&-I_2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\alpha_j=\\begin{pmatrix}0&\\sigma_j\\\\\\sigma_j&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\gamma^0=\\beta,\\qquad \\gamma^j=\\beta\\alpha_j.$$\nFor a spinor column $z\\in\\mathbb C^4$, its Dirac adjoint is $\\bar z=z^\\dagger\\beta$. The Clifford contraction is $\\Gamma(p):=\\gamma^0p^0-\\sum_{j=1}^3\\gamma^jp_j$; this contraction uses the effective matrix $-\\eta$ in the relation $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$.\n\nLet $\\mathcal H_D$ be the space of measurable functions $u:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb C^4$, modulo equality almost everywhere, with\n$$\\|u\\|^2=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}u(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger u(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}<\\infty,$$\nand pairing $\\langle u,v\\rangle=\\int u(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger v(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\pi)^3$. Define the measurable matrix $h_D(\\mathbf k)=\\sum_j\\alpha_j k_j+\\beta\\mu$ and its maximal multiplication operator with domain $D(h_D)=\\{u\\in\\mathcal H_D:h_Du\\in\\mathcal H_D\\}$. For $\\omega(\\mathbf k)>0$, define the matrix fields\n$$P_\\pm(\\mathbf k)=\\frac12\\left(I_4\\pm\\frac{h_D(\\mathbf k)}{\\omega(\\mathbf k)}\\right).$$\nWhen $\\mu=0$ at $\\mathbf k=0$, set $P_+(0)=\\operatorname{diag}(I_2,0)$ and $P_-(0)=\\operatorname{diag}(0,I_2)$. These are complementary rank-two orthogonal projections; the value at this Lebesgue-null point does not change their multiplication operators. Write $M_{P_\\pm}$ for their maximal multiplication operators and $\\mathcal H_\\pm=\\operatorname{ran}M_{P_\\pm}$. The companion matrix lemma on this page proves these are bounded orthogonal projections with closed ranges. The conjugate Hilbert space $\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$ has vectors $\\bar u$, scalar multiplication $z\\bar u=\\overline{\\bar z u}$, and pairing $\\langle\\bar u,\\bar v\\rangle=\\langle v,u\\rangle$.\n\nUse the sine and cosine definitions of [[def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series]]. For a length-coordinate increment $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\omega(\\mathbf k)>0$, define the matrix multiplier\n$$W_D(t,\\mathbf k)=\\cos(\\omega t)I_4+i\\frac{\\sin(\\omega t)}{\\omega}h_D(\\mathbf k).$$\nAt the massless apex set $W_D(t,0)=I_4$. Its unitarity, group law and generator identity are proved by the companion matrix lemma on this page.\n\nThe symbols $P_+$ and $P_-$ designate the positive- and negative-energy matrix formulas for this fixed free Dirac operator. This is purely mathematical data; no physical spin-statistics postulate or empirical assertion is included.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "2.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] and use the definitions in [[def-qft-dirac-projections]]. For every $\\mu\\ge0$ and almost every $\\mathbf k$, the displayed Pauli blocks satisfy $h_D(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger=h_D(\\mathbf k)$, $h_D(\\mathbf k)^2=\\omega(\\mathbf k)^2I_4$, and $P_\\pm(\\mathbf k)$ are complementary orthogonal projections of rank two. The ranges $\\mathcal H_\\pm$ are closed separable Hilbert subspaces of $\\mathcal H_D$, and $h_D$ is self-adjoint on $D(h_D)=\\{u:\\omega u\\in\\mathcal H_D\\}$. The matrices also satisfy $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$ and $\\gamma^{0\\dagger}=\\gamma^0$, $\\gamma^{j\\dagger}=-\\gamma^j$.\n\nFor a unit spatial vector $\\mathbf n$, put $\\Sigma_{\\mathbf n}=\\operatorname{diag}(\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\cdot\\mathbf n,\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\cdot\\mathbf n)$, $S_R(\\theta)=\\cos(\\theta/2)I_4-i\\sin(\\theta/2)\\Sigma_{\\mathbf n}$, and $S_B(\\chi)=\\cosh(\\chi/2)I_4+\\sinh(\\chi/2)\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbf n$. With $R_{\\mathbf n}(\\theta)$ the rotation $\\mathbf v_\\parallel+\\cos\\theta\\,\\mathbf v_\\perp+\\sin\\theta\\,\\mathbf n\\times\\mathbf v_\\perp$ using [[def-cross-product-in-r3]], with $\\mathbf v_\\parallel=(\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf n)\\mathbf n$ and $\\mathbf v_\\perp=\\mathbf v-\\mathbf v_\\parallel$, and $B_{\\mathbf n}(\\chi)$ the boost defined in [[lem-qft-lorentz-boost-rotation-factorization]], these matrices satisfy $S_R\\Gamma(p)S_R^{-1}=\\Gamma(R_{\\mathbf n}(\\theta)p)$, $S_B\\Gamma(p)S_B^{-1}=\\Gamma(B_{\\mathbf n}(\\chi)p)$, and $S_R\\beta S_R^\\dagger=S_B\\beta S_B^\\dagger=\\beta$.\n\nFor each real $t$, $W_D(t,\\mathbf k)$ of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]] is unitary, satisfies $W_D(t+s,\\mathbf k)=W_D(t,\\mathbf k)W_D(s,\\mathbf k)$, and obeys $\\partial_tW_D(t,\\mathbf k)=i h_D(\\mathbf k)W_D(t,\\mathbf k)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "2.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] and let $H$ be a complex\nHilbert space with the second-variable-linear convention of\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. On $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ define the bosonic\ncreation and annihilation maps $a^\\dagger(h)$ and $a(h)$ and the fermionic maps\n$c^\\dagger(h)$ and $c(h)$ as in\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. Write $a_n(h)$ and $a_n^\\dagger(h)$ for\ntheir restrictions to the $n$th bosonic sector. For bosons set\n\n$$D(a(h))=\\{\\Psi\\in\\mathcal F_s(H):\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\|a_n(h)\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\},\\qquad D(a^\\dagger(h))=\\{\\Psi\\in\\mathcal F_s(H):\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\|a_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}.$$\n\nThen $\\mathcal F_s(H)$ and $\\mathcal F_a(H)$ are Hilbert spaces,\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is dense, and it is a graph core for both maximal\nbosonic sector maps. The bosonic operators on their displayed domains are\nclosed and mutually adjoint, and on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ satisfy\n\n$$[a(h),a^\\dagger(k)]=\\langle h,k\\rangle_2I,\\qquad [a(h),a(k)]=[a^\\dagger(h),a^\\dagger(k)]=0.$$\n\nThe fermionic $c(h)$ and $c^\\dagger(h)$ extend to bounded operators on the\nwhole Fock space, with norms $\\|h\\|$, and satisfy on the whole space\n\n$$\\{c(h),c^\\dagger(k)\\}=\\langle h,k\\rangle_2I,\\qquad \\{c(h),c(k)\\}=\\{c^\\dagger(h),c^\\dagger(k)\\}=0.$$\n\nThis is a mathematical construction of the two Fock representations; it does\nnot assign particle statistics to a physical species.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-fourier-transform-agrees-with-l-one-and-plancherel-transforms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and use the negative-sign $2\\pi$ normalization.  If\n$f\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)$, then\n\n$$\\mathcal F u_f=u_{\\widehat f},$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat f$ is the integral Fourier transform.  If\n$f\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)$, then\n\n$$\\mathcal F u_f=u_{\\mathcal F_2f},$$\n\nwhere $\\mathcal F_2$ is the Plancherel extension.  These equalities are in\n$\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^n)$ and therefore depend only on the corresponding\nalmost-everywhere classes.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Here\n$\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ is the Schwartz space of smooth complex functions\nwhose coordinate-weighted derivatives are all bounded, as defined in\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]].\nFix an oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski space and an inertial coordinate\nchart as in [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]. Use coordinates\n$x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$ and metric $\\eta=\\mathrm{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, matching the\n$(-+++)$ convention of the SR suppliers. Some QFT sources use its negative\n$(+---)$; the Lorentz group is unchanged, and the phase is fixed explicitly\nbelow.\nCoordinates $x$ have length units, momenta have inverse-length units, and the\nFourier phase below is dimensionless. A parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ has inverse-length\nunits and is only a mathematical shell parameter; it is not identified with a\nphysical rest mass. For $\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3$, set\n$$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2},\\qquad p_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k).$$\nThe future mass shell is\n$$H_\\mu^+=\\{(p^0,\\mathbf p): (p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2=\\mu^2,\\ p^0>0\\}.$$\nWhen $\\mu=0$, its apex is omitted; thus $\\mathbf k\\ne0$ in the massless\nparameterization. The scalar shell pairing is\n$$p\\circ x:=p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x=-\\eta(p,x).$$\nDefine the measure and one-particle Hilbert space by\n$$d\\nu_\\mu(p_\\mu(\\mathbf k))= \\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)},\\qquad H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu;\\mathbb C),$$\nwhere the omitted massless apex has measure zero. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb\nR^4)$ define its pointwise shell Fourier restriction by\n$$K_\\mu f(p)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}f(x)e^{ip\\circ x}\\,d^4x.$$\nThe Fourier convention is exactly the displayed integral, with no prefactor.\nIf $\\widehat f(\\xi^0,\\boldsymbol\\xi)=\n\\int f(x)e^{-2\\pi i(x^0\\xi^0+\\mathbf x\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\xi)}\\,d^4x$ denotes\nthe standard transform in\n[[cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space]],\nthen\n$$\\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p):=\\int f(x)e^{i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)}\\,d^4x =\\widehat f\\!\\left(-\\frac{p^0}{2\\pi},\\frac{\\mathbf p}{2\\pi}\\right).$$\nIts inverse is\n$$f(x)=(2\\pi)^{-4}\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} \\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p)e^{-i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)} \\,dp^0\\,d^3\\mathbf p.$$\nThus this convention is a time reflection and rescaling of the cited\nSchwartz automorphism and has the stated reciprocal normalization.\nThe proper orthochronous Lorentz group consists of linear maps preserving\n$\\eta$, orientation and the chosen future cone. A Poincare map is\n$x\\mapsto a+\\Lambda x$ with $a\\in\\mathbb R^4$ and $\\Lambda$ in that group.\n\nThis is a mathematical free scalar one-particle construction. It adopts the\nchosen coordinates, measure and Fourier convention; it asserts no experimental\nobservation, physical mass identification or measurement outcome.",
          "uses": [
            "2.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. For $\\mu\\ge0$, define the tempered distribution\n$$\\Delta_\\mu(t,\\mathbf x)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3} \\frac{\\sin(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)t)}{\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)} e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x},$$\nwith the massless integrand defined off $\\mathbf k=0$; its $1/|\\mathbf k|$\nbehavior is locally integrable in three dimensions. Then\n$$\\operatorname{supp}\\Delta_\\mu \\subseteq\\{(t,\\mathbf x):t^2-|\\mathbf x|^2\\ge0\\},$$\nthe closed causal cone for the $(-+++)$ metric. For real compactly supported\nsmooth $f,g$, put\n$$\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} f(x)g(y)\\Delta_\\mu(x-y)\\,d^4x\\,d^4y.$$\nOn $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ the smeared fields of\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]] satisfy\n$$[\\Phi_\\mu(f),\\Phi_\\mu(g)]=-i\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)I.$$\nConsequently spacelike-separated supports give commuting fields.",
          "uses": [
            "2.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "An **oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space** is a quadruple\n$(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ with the following data. In this notation $V$ includes its\nspecified orientation; forgetting that orientation gives only the underlying\nreal vector space, not the full datum $V$ of the model.\n\n1. A nonempty affine space $A$ over a $4$-dimensional real vector space $V$,\n   with action $(p,v)\\mapsto p+v$ and displacement $q-p\\in V$\n   ([[def-affine-space-and-displacement]]). In particular $V$ is a real vector\n   space of dimension $4$.\n2. A Lorentz form $\\eta$ on $V$, i.e. a nondegenerate symmetric bilinear form of\n   inertia $(3,1,0)$ ([[def-lorentz-form-and-inertia]]). Its associated quadratic\n   form is $q(v)=\\eta(v,v)$.\n3. An **orientation** of $V$: a positive ray of the determinant line\n   $\\Lambda^4V$, fixed once and for all\n   ([[def-determinant-line-orientation-of-a-finite-dimensional-real-vector-space]]).\n4. A **time orientation** $C^+$ of $(V,\\eta)$: one of the two connected\n   components of the timelike set $T=\\{v\\in V:\\eta(v,v)<0\\}$, the other being\n   $C^-:=T\\setminus C^+$ ([[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]]).\n\nThe following terminology is attached to such a quadruple.\n\n- The elements $p$ of $A$ are the **events**; for events $p,q$ the vector\n  $q-p\\in V$ is the **displacement from $p$ to $q$**.\n- The **interval** of $p,q\\in A$ is the real number\n  $I(p,q):=\\eta(q-p,q-p)=q(q-p)$.\n- Events $p,q$ are **timelike**, **null**, **spacelike** or **causally\n  related** when the displacement $q-p$ is timelike, null, spacelike or causal\n  in the sense of [[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]]; they are\n  **future-related** when $q-p$ is future causal and **past-related** when\n  $q-p$ is past causal in the sense of\n  [[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]].\n\n**No point of $A$ is distinguished.** For every $v\\in V$ the translation\n$\\tau_v:A\\to A$, $\\tau_v(p):=p+v$, is a bijection, since it is inverse to\n$\\tau_{-v}$: $\\tau_{-v}(\\tau_v(p))=(p+v)+(-v)=p+(v-v)=p$ and symmetrically, by\nthe affine action axioms ([[def-affine-space-and-displacement]]). Displacements\nare translation invariant, $\\tau_v(q)-\\tau_v(p)=q-p$, so every $\\tau_v$ preserves\nthe interval, the causal classification of every ordered pair, and the sets\n$C^+$, $C^-$ (which are subsets of $V$, independent of the point of $A$); it\nchanges only the labelling of points. The mathematical structure of the model is\nexactly the quadruple $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$; no derived physical quantity (proper\ntime, four-velocity, momentum, energy, charge or any field) is part of this\ndefinition, and all later structure of the framework (frames, charts, tensor\nfields, worldlines) is built on this model.\n\n**Well-definedness.** The affine-space definition supplies the action and the\nunique displacements; the Lorentz-form definition supplies $\\eta$ and its\ninertia; the determinant-line definition supplies orientations; and the two\ncomponents of $T$ between which the time-orientation definition chooses exist by\nthe components lemma recorded in the well-definedness clause of\n[[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]], which is also what makes the\nnames future and past causal in item 4 well defined.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be an oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space\n([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]), with orientation fixed as a positive ray of\nthe determinant line $\\Lambda^4V$\n([[def-determinant-line-orientation-of-a-finite-dimensional-real-vector-space]])\nand with the corresponding class of positively oriented ordered bases of $V$\n([[def-orientation-of-a-finite-dimensional-real-vector-space]]); by\n[[prop-orientations-and-positive-basis-classes-agree-in-positive-dimension]] the\npositive ray selects exactly one such class for $\\dim V=4>0$.\n\nAn **inertial frame** of the model is a tuple $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ consisting\nof an event $o\\in A$ and an ordered basis $(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of $V$ that is\n\n1. **$\\eta$-orthonormal**: $\\eta(e_a,e_b)=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)_{ab}$ for all $a,b$ ([[lem-lorentz-form-has-orthonormal-basis]]);\n2. **future**: $e_0\\in C^+$, i.e. $e_0$ is future timelike; and\n3. **positively oriented**: the ordered basis lies in the positive-basis class\n   selected by the fixed orientation.\n\nThe event $o$ is the **origin** of the frame and $e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3$ are its\n**basis vectors**.\n\n**Inertial charts.** For an inertial frame $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$, define\n\n$$E:\\mathbb R^4\\longrightarrow V,\\qquad E(u):=\\sum_{a=0}^3u^ae_a .$$\n\nThen $E$ is a linear isomorphism ([[def-linear-isomorphism-and-invertible-linear-map]]):\nit is linear by construction, and it is bijective because\n$(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ is an ordered basis, every $v\\in V$ having exactly one\ncoordinate list $u$ with $v=\\sum_au^ae_a$\n([[thm-unique-coordinates-with-respect-to-an-ordered-basis]]). The **inertial\nchart** of the frame is the map\n\n$$x:A\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^4,\\qquad x(p):=E^{-1}(p-o),$$\n\nand its values $x^a(p)$ are the **inertial coordinates of the event $p$** in the\nframe. Since $E$ and $v\\mapsto p+v$ are bijections, $x$ is a bijection\n$A\\to\\mathbb R^4$; and $x(p)=0$ exactly when $p-o=0_V$, that is, exactly when\n$p=o$.\n\n**Transition law.** Let $(o';e'_0,e'_1,e'_2,e'_3)$ be another inertial frame\nwith chart $x'$, and let $M=(M^b_a)$ be the change-of-basis matrix defined by\n$e'_a=\\sum_bM^b_ae_b$, a $4\\times4$ real matrix that is invertible because both\nfamilies are ordered bases. Then $E'(u)=\\sum_au^ae'_a=E(Mu)$ for all\n$u\\in\\mathbb R^4$, for the matrix-vector product $Mu$ in the coordinates of the\nfirst frame, because $\\sum_au^a\\sum_bM^b_ae_b=\\sum_b(\\sum_aM^b_au^a)e_b$; hence\n$E'=E\\circ M$ and $E'^{-1}=M^{-1}\\circ E^{-1}$, so for every event $p$,\n\n$$x'(p)=M^{-1}\\bigl(x(p)-x(o')\\bigr)=M^{-1}x(p)-M^{-1}x(o') .$$\n\nIn particular the transition between two inertial charts is an affine map of\n$\\mathbb R^4$ with invertible linear part $M^{-1}$ and constant part\n$-M^{-1}x(o')$, and it is a bijection of $\\mathbb R^4$ with inverse\n$u\\mapsto Mu+x(o')$, which is the transition in the reverse direction.\n\n**The inertial atlas.** The **inertial atlas** of the model is the family of\nall inertial frames together with their inertial charts. Its charts are\navailable at every event by [[lem-future-oriented-inertial-frames-exist]], which\nsupplies for every $o\\in A$ an inertial frame with origin $o$; and any two of\nits charts are related by the affine transition displayed above.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to\n$L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then:\n\n1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$;\n2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$;\n3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the\n   section-integral functions are integrable; and\n4. the three integrals agree:\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
