          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] and use the definitions in [[def-qft-dirac-projections]]. For every $\\mu\\ge0$ and almost every $\\mathbf k$, the displayed Pauli blocks satisfy $h_D(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger=h_D(\\mathbf k)$, $h_D(\\mathbf k)^2=\\omega(\\mathbf k)^2I_4$, and $P_\\pm(\\mathbf k)$ are complementary orthogonal projections of rank two. The ranges $\\mathcal H_\\pm$ are closed separable Hilbert subspaces of $\\mathcal H_D$, and $h_D$ is self-adjoint on $D(h_D)=\\{u:\\omega u\\in\\mathcal H_D\\}$. The matrices also satisfy $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$ and $\\gamma^{0\\dagger}=\\gamma^0$, $\\gamma^{j\\dagger}=-\\gamma^j$.\n\nFor a unit spatial vector $\\mathbf n$, put $\\Sigma_{\\mathbf n}=\\operatorname{diag}(\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\cdot\\mathbf n,\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\cdot\\mathbf n)$, $S_R(\\theta)=\\cos(\\theta/2)I_4-i\\sin(\\theta/2)\\Sigma_{\\mathbf n}$, and $S_B(\\chi)=\\cosh(\\chi/2)I_4+\\sinh(\\chi/2)\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbf n$. With $R_{\\mathbf n}(\\theta)$ the rotation $\\mathbf v_\\parallel+\\cos\\theta\\,\\mathbf v_\\perp+\\sin\\theta\\,\\mathbf n\\times\\mathbf v_\\perp$ using [[def-cross-product-in-r3]], with $\\mathbf v_\\parallel=(\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf n)\\mathbf n$ and $\\mathbf v_\\perp=\\mathbf v-\\mathbf v_\\parallel$, and $B_{\\mathbf n}(\\chi)$ the boost defined in [[lem-qft-lorentz-boost-rotation-factorization]], these matrices satisfy $S_R\\Gamma(p)S_R^{-1}=\\Gamma(R_{\\mathbf n}(\\theta)p)$, $S_B\\Gamma(p)S_B^{-1}=\\Gamma(B_{\\mathbf n}(\\chi)p)$, and $S_R\\beta S_R^\\dagger=S_B\\beta S_B^\\dagger=\\beta$.\n\nFor each real $t$, $W_D(t,\\mathbf k)$ of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]] is unitary, satisfies $W_D(t+s,\\mathbf k)=W_D(t,\\mathbf k)W_D(s,\\mathbf k)$, and obeys $\\partial_tW_D(t,\\mathbf k)=i h_D(\\mathbf k)W_D(t,\\mathbf k)$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(V,\\eta)$ be a $4$-dimensional Lorentz vector space with a chosen time\norientation $C^+$, and let $L:V\\to V$ be a linear map with\n$\\eta(Lv,Lw)=\\eta(v,w)$ for all $v,w\\in V$\n([[def-lorentz-map-and-lorentz-group]]). Then:\n\n(i) $L$ is a linear isomorphism of $V$; for every ordered $\\eta$-orthonormal\nbasis the matrix $M$ of $L$ satisfies $M^{T}\\eta_0M=\\eta_0$ with\n$\\eta_0=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and conversely every invertible matrix\n$M$ with $M^{T}\\eta_0M=\\eta_0$ is the matrix of a linear Lorentz map in that\nbasis.\n\n(ii) $\\det L=\\pm1$. The composition of two Lorentz maps is a Lorentz map, the\nidentity is a Lorentz map, and the inverse of a Lorentz map is a Lorentz map;\nthe sets $SO(V,\\eta):=\\{L\\in O(V,\\eta):\\det L=1\\}$,\n$O^{\\uparrow}(V,\\eta):=\\{L\\in O(V,\\eta):L(C^+)=C^+\\}$ and\n$SO^{+}(V,\\eta)=SO(V,\\eta)\\cap O^{\\uparrow}(V,\\eta)$ are subgroups of\n$O(V,\\eta)$, which itself is a subgroup of the general linear group $GL(V)$.\n\n(iii) $\\eta(Lv,Lv)=\\eta(v,v)$ for all $v\\in V$; hence $L$ preserves the causal\ncharacter of every nonzero vector (timelike, null or spacelike) and maps the\nset of null vectors onto itself. If $L$ is proper, then the oriented volume\nform is preserved:\n$\\epsilon(Lv_0,\\dots,Lv_3)=\\epsilon(v_0,\\dots,v_3)$ for all\n$v_0,\\dots,v_3\\in V$ ([[def-oriented-minkowski-volume-form]]); if\n$\\det L=-1$ the two sides differ by the sign $-1$.\n\n(iv) $L(C^+)$ is one of the two components $C^+,C^-$ of the timelike set, so\nexactly one of $L(C^+)=C^+$ and $L(C^+)=C^-$ holds; $L$ is orthochronous in\nthe first case and non-orthochronous in the second, and $L(C^+)=C^+$ holds if\nand only if $L(C^+)$ is contained in $C^+$.\n\n(v) The affine Lorentz maps of\n[[def-lorentz-map-and-lorentz-group]] form a group under composition: the\ncomposition and the inverse of affine Lorentz maps are affine Lorentz maps,\nand every affine Lorentz map is a bijection of $A$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
